  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nShowers and thunderstorms return to the forecast this weekend, \nwith the possibility of 1-3 inches of rain and a few \nstrong/severe storms.\n\nA storm system, anchored by an area of low pressure over eastern \nIowa, is expected to slowly move southeast toward the Ohio Valley \ntoday. Moisture is expected to rapidly deepen across the forecast \narea Saturday ahead of this system, with precipitable water values \nlikely reaching somewhere between 1.5 and 2 inches by Saturday \nevening. To put this in somewhat of a climatological perspective, \nthese values are approximately 2 standard deviations above normal. \nAs the shortwave trough drives the low closer to our region, \ndisturbances will move through the moist airmass in place over our \narea and lead to the development of showers and thunderstorms. We \ncould see an isolated to scattered smattering of showers and storms \nas early as this afternoon, primarily across western North Carolina. \nConvective development will become more likely, though, after \nmidnight, with a greater coverage anticipated through Sunday night.\n\nBy Sunday, we'll primarily be watching for the development of two \npotential weather impacts across our region. \n\nThe first thing is heavy rainfall, especially given how moist we \nexpect the airmass to be across the forecast area. The storm system \nwill be rather slow-moving, allowing a series of disturbances to \nmove across the region. The development of off-and-on showers and \nstorms over similar areas could result in localized ponding/flooding \nissues. As such, WPC has our area outlined for a Marginal, or Level \n1 Risk, of excessive rainfall for Sunday. \n\nThe second thing is the possibility of isolated strong or severe \nthunderstorms. The right entrance region of the upper jet should be \nmoving across our neck of the woods Sunday afternoon, resulting in \nincreased diffluence aloft. Thermodynamically, mixed-layer CAPE \nvalues around 1000 J/kg (maybe a little more) could seen, especially \nfrom Southside Virginia into the North Carolina Piedmont. With the \nincreased forcing provided by the surface low and cold front to our \nwest, we could see some isolated strong or severe storms in this \nenvironment. There are a couple of limiting factors in my mind... \n(1) Boundary layer wind speeds might be a tad low to get significant \nstorm-scale organization. (2) If precipitation is more widespread \nthan currently forecast, then instability should be lower. Given the \noverall risk, SPC has our area outlined for a Marginal Risk (Level \n1) for seeing severe storms Sunday with the primary impact being \ndamaging winds.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nUnsettled weather continues through the front half of the week,\nwith a possible decrease in convective coverage at the end of the\nweek.\n\nAs an upper-level low settles into the Tennessee Valley, it should \nslowly push a cold front through most of our area before it stalls. \nThe upper low is then expected to \"sit and spin\" close to the \nforecast area through much of the upcoming week. This feature will \ncontinue to send disturbances through a rather moist airmass, \nresulting in continued odds of seeing daily showers and \nthunderstorms. There's some indication that convective probabilities \nmay decrease during the latter half of the week as the upper low \nejects toward the Northeast U.S.",
